Scenario-Based Linux Interview Questions & Answers.

1️⃣ High CPU Usage
Q: A production server is running slow. How would you identify and troubleshoot high CPU usage?
 Answer:
 • Use top, htop, vmstat, or pidstat
 • Identify high-CPU processes: ps -eo pid,ppid,cmd,%mem,%cpu --sort=-%cpu
 • Check cron jobs and application logs
 • Kill or restart misbehaving processes if needed
 • Investigate memory and swap usage
2️⃣ Disk Space Full
Q: Application fails to write logs due to full disk. How to resolve?
 Answer:
 • Check usage: df -h and du -sh /*
 • Find large files: find / -type f -size +500M
 • Clean /var/log and rotate logs using logrotate
 • Clear package cache: yum clean all / apt clean
 • Check deleted open files: lsof | grep deleted
3️⃣ Service Not Starting
Q: systemctl start nginx fails. How to debug?
 Answer:
 • Check service status: systemctl status nginx
 • View logs: journalctl -xe or journalctl -u nginx
 • Verify config: /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
 • Check port conflicts: netstat -tulnp or ss -ltnp
 • Check SELinux or permission issues
4️⃣ SSH Login Failure
Q: Can't SSH into server. What to check first?
 Answer:
 • Network reachability: ping, telnet, nc
 • SSH port (22) open? ss -tuln
 • Check sshd service: systemctl status sshd
 • Review logs: /var/log/auth.log or /var/log/secure
 • Check .ssh/authorized_keys permissions and ownership
5️⃣ File Permission Issue
Q: Script runs manually but fails in cron. Why?
 Answer:
 • Cron has limited $PATH and environment variables
 • Use absolute paths in scripts
 • Check user permissions: chmod, chown
 • Redirect output for debugging: script.sh >> /tmp/cron.log 2>&1
6️⃣ Crontab Not Working
Q: Scheduled cron job isn’t executing. How to debug?
 Answer:
 • Check crontab: crontab -l / crontab -e
 • Review logs: /var/log/cron or /var/log/syslog
 • Validate syntax: crontab.guru

 • Ensure script is executable and has correct shebang
7️⃣ Port Already in Use
Q: Web server fails to bind to port 80. Solution?
 Answer:
 • Identify process: netstat -tulnp or ss -ltnp
 • Kill or reconfigure conflicting service
 • Trace PID: lsof -i :80
 • Ensure process runs as root (ports < 1024 require root)
8️⃣ Kernel Panic or Server Crash
Q: Server rebooted unexpectedly. How to investigate?
 Answer:
 • Check logs: /var/log/messages, /var/crash, journalctl
 • Inspect kernel messages: dmesg
 • Look for memory/disk errors
 • Enable kdump for future crash analysis
9️⃣ Symbolic vs Hard Links
Q: Symbolic link breaks after moving target file. Why?
 Answer:
 • Symlinks point to file path, moving file breaks the link
 • Hard links point to the inode, moving file doesn’t break it
 • Commands: ln -s (symbolic), ln (hard).
